    <p>
      What I have done here is called a simulation. In general, there
      are two main ways to determine the outcomes of a (discrete)
      probability situation.
      <ul>
        <li>
          I can use mathematical theory to predict and prove what the
          probabilities will be for various possibilities in a
          probability situation.
        </li>
        <li>
          I can run a simulation, basically repeating the probability
          situation over and over again, and use a histogram to record
          the probability of all the various outcomes.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
    <p>
      In the case of rolling two six-sided dice, after two hundred
      rolls, the histogram of outcomes was starting to look quite
      similar to the histogram-like graph of probabilities predicted
      by the theorey. The most times I roll two dice, the closer this
      similarity will grow. In general, we expect this to happen.
    </p>
    <theorem>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <term>The Law of Large Numbers</term> is a colloquial term
          for the fact that, as the number of iterations grows,
          simulations of probability get closer and closer to matching
          a correct theory of the probability situation.  
        </p>
      </statement>
    </theorem>
    <p>
      Basically, if you repeat a probability situation enough times,
      you can get extremely precise and accurate versions of the
      probability. The simtulations and the theory converge. This is
      very useful. Probability theory is a well-developed part of
      mathematics, but it’s a pretty difficult discipline. With enough
      capacity (usually computing power) we can avoid a lot of the
      theory by just running simulations. This is actually done:
      simulations are a huge part of the development of the theory of
      probability. Thanks to the Law of Large Numbers, simulations can
      be used to predict or test the theory of probability.
    </p>
    <p>
      For our purposes, in statistics we can use simulations to have
      efficient way to get accurate assessments of the probabilities
      in a probability situation. We will use this to determine
      probabilities for hypothesis testing.
    </p>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsection-continuous-probability">
    <title>Continuous Probability</title>
    <p>
      Discrete probability is about discrete possibilities, like the
      outcomes of a die roll. Continuous probability is, in contrast,
      about continuous possibilities, like temperature, weight,
      lifespan, or other quantities which could, in theory, have
      aribtray precious. However, continouus probability has a whole
      set of very useful techinque. These are useful enough that it is
      often helpful to use continuous probability to model a discrete
      situation. Let me go back to the dice rolle example. <xref
      ref="figure-2d6-6"/> is the same probability graph from the
      simulation of 500 dice throws, but now I have added a continuous
      function that matches the probability.
