    </figure>
    <p>
      Obviously, a dice roll cannot fall between whole numbers. But
      the continuous model is still useful. I want to understand how
      to measure probability in the continuous model. To do so, think
      about the bars in this probability graph. The height of the
      graph is the probability, but notice that the width of each bar
      is exactly 1. This means that the area of each bar is the
      probability, height, times the width, which is <m>1</m>. To the
      area is also a precise measure of the probability. For
      continuous probability, this is the important insight. Now let
      me give you the definitions we will use.
    </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Consider a continuous probability situation where the
          possibilities are a range of (real) numbers <m>[a,b]</m>. 
          Then a probability distribution is a mathematical function
          <m>p(x)</m> with the following properties.
          <ul>
            <li>
              The domain of the function is the range of possibilities
              <m>[a,b]</m>.
            </li>
            <li>
              All outcomes of the function at all positive: <m>p(x)
              \geq 0</m>. 
            </li>
            <li>
              The total are under the function, for the whole domain,
              is exactly <m>1</m>.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      For discrete probability, the possibilities and their
      probabilities determine everything. For continuous probability,
      the function, the probability distribution, determines
      everything. How does it do so? By area.
    </p>
    <proposition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>p(x)</m> be a probability distribution on domain
          <m>[a,b]</m>.  Let <m>[c.d]</m> be a subset of the
          possibilites (so that it is an interval inside
          <m>[a,b]</m>). Then the probability of having any
          possibility between <m>c</m> an d<m>d</m> is precisely the
          area under the probability distribution that falls between
          <m>c</m> and <m>d</m>. 
        </p>
      </statement>
    </proposition>
    <p>
      For a continuous probability situation, area is probability.
      <xref ref="figure-continuous-probability1"/> and <xref
      ref="figure-continuous-probability2"/> show how this looks: the
      probability under the whole distribution, shown in blue, is
      <m>1</m>. The probability of a sub-range of possibilities is the
      area of the smaller section, shown in the red, which will be
      smaller than the blue area. In the figure, the area of the red
      region  is the probability of a possibility between <m>4</m> and
      <m>8</m>, as a fraction of the whole probability between
      <m>0</m> and <m>10</m>. Note that all the possibilities are
      values on the <m>x</m>-axis: in a probability distribution, the
      <m>x</m>-axis is always the possible outcomes of the probability
      situation.
    </p>
    <figure xml:id="figure-continuous-probability1">
      <caption>A Probability Distribution</caption>
      <image source="images/image1.png" width="80%">
      </image>
    </figure>
    <figure xml:id="figure-continuous-probability2">
      <caption>
        Probablity of an outcome between <m>4</m> and <m>8</m>
      </caption>
      <image source="images/image2.png" width="80%">
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>
      Finally, it would be nice to have a good notation for this area.
      To that end, I’m going to give you a definition from calculus.
      For those who are not familiar with calculus, please just treat
      this as a piece of new notation. I will not ask you to calculate
      this — I just want you to understand this notation. (For those
      who do have some calculus, I hope you enjoy the connections
      here.) 
    </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The integral of a function <m>p(x)</m> over an interval
          <m>[a.b]</m> is a measure of the area under the graph of
          <m>p(x)</m> between the points <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>. It has
          this notation. 
          <md>
            \int_a^b p(x) dx 
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      Continuous probability is, indeed, a subdiscipline of calculus.
      Calculus is not required for this statistics course, so I will
      be very minimal with uses of calculus. Hopefully the notation is
      not to strange.
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>
